{"payload":{"candidate":{"prompt":"You are given a JSON input with two parts: 'system_profile' (the installed software inventory, with product names and versions) and 'advisories' (a supplied list of CVE entries, each with an ID, description, and affected-version information).\n\nYour task: determine which of the SUPPLIED advisories actually apply to the INSTALLED system, and return exactly:\n{\"applicable_cves\":[\"CVE-...\"]}\n\nCRITICAL RULES:\n\n1. ONLY use CVE IDs that appear in the supplied advisories list. Never invent, guess, or construct IDs. Deduplicate the output array.\n\n2. MATCHING THE PRODUCT:\n- An advisory applies only if the affected product is actually installed on the system. The advisory's product name may be written differently from the inventory name: vendors may be prefixed or stripped (e.g., 'Apache Log4j' vs 'log4j'), names may be title-case vs lowercase, and package-manager aliases (e.g., 'libssl-dev', 'openssl', 'nodejs') may be used. Normalize names (lowercase, strip vendor prefixes and package-manager decorations) and match on the core product name.\n- Do NOT mark advisories for products that are not installed, even if descriptions mention them.\n\n3. MATCHING THE VERSION — this is where most errors occur. Be rigorous:\n- Compare versions as NUMERIC SEGMENT-BY-SEGMENT, not lexicographically. '1.10.0' is GREATER than '1.9.0' (10 > 9, not string comparison). Pad shorter versions with zeros when comparing ('2.1' == '2.1.0').\n- Advisories may state affected ranges as structured fields (e.g., 'affected_versions', 'version_start_including', 'version_end_excluding', 'version_end_including') or in PROSE within the description (e.g., 'versions prior to X', 'versions up to and including X', 'affects X through Y', 'versions before X.Y.Z are affected'). Parse both forms.\n- Pay close attention to whether bounds are INCLUSIVE or EXCLUSIVE. 'up to and including', 'through', 'version_end_including' means the boundary version ITSELF is affected. 'prior to', 'before', 'version_end_excluding' means the boundary version is NOT affected. 'on or before' is inclusive; 'less than' is exclusive.\n- If the installed version equals an inclusive bound, the advisory APPLIES.\n- If the installed version equals an exclusive bound, the advisory DOES NOT apply.\n- If the installed version is outside the stated affected range (above the upper bound or below the lower bound), the advisory DOES NOT apply — even if the product is installed.\n- Single-segment versions (e.g., '3') compare as '3.0.0.0'. Do not misjudge bounds because of differing segment counts.\n- If an advisory lists multiple affected versions/ranges, it applies if the installed version falls in ANY of them.\n- If an advisory states no version information for an installed product, treat it as applicable only if the product is clearly the affected one.\n\n4. DECISION PROCEDURE (follow for every advisory):\n(a) Identify the affected product; check if it is installed. If not installed → not applicable.\n(b) Extract the exact affected version range(s) from structured fields and/or the description text.\n(c) Compare the INSTALLED version numerically against the range, respecting inclusive/exclusive bounds and segment padding.\n(d) Include the advisory's ID only if the installed version is inside an affected range.\n\n5. OUTPUT: Return ONLY the JSON object {\"applicable_cves\":[...]} containing the unique IDs of all applicable advisories, or {\"applicable_cves\":[]} if none apply. No explanations, no extra fields, no markdown — just the JSON object.\n\nBefore finalizing, re-verify each included ID: product installed? version within bounds with correct inclusivity? And re-verify each excluded ID for an installed product: is the installed version truly outside the range, or was the bound misinterpreted (inclusive vs exclusive) or miscompared (segment count, numeric vs string)?"},"handle":"5cc1d5b0764090d40e21e42e6abc06d752d14dbbe1644581ea3cac4dfe56bd50","profile_ids":["opti